<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kallanai Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Grand Anicut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kallanai, or Grand Anicut, is an ancient dam across the Kaveri River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Tamil tradition credits its construction to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king Karikala in the early centuries CE, making it one of the oldest water-diversion works still in use. It was rebuilt and enlarged by British engineers in the 19th century and still feeds the irrigation of the Kaveri delta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kallanai-dam.jpg" alt="Kallanai Dam" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ashwin Kumar from Bangalore, India · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Anicut,_Kallanai_(11876067114).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dam is dated to about 100-150 CE. It was built in running water, to divert the Kaveri into the delta districts and so increase irrigation there. In 1804 the British appointed Captain Caldwell, a military engineer, to study the river; finding that much of its water passed into the Kollidam, leaving little for irrigation, he raised the dam's stones by 69 centimetres. Major Sim then proposed undersluices with outlets to the Kollidam to prevent silting. The Lower Anaicut built across the Kollidam by Sir Arthur Cotton in the 19th century is said to be modelled on the Kallanai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Kallanai stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> district, about 45 km from the city of Thanjavur. Some 32 km to its west the Kaveri divides into two channels, which enclose the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a> before meeting again at the dam. The northern channel is the Kollidam, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollidam-river">Coleroon</a>; the other keeps the name Kaveri and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puhar">Poompuhar</a>. The dam is built of unhewn stone and is 329 m long, 20 m wide and 5.4 m high, and it is still in excellent condition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Below the barrage the river splits into four streams, the Kollidam Aru, the Kaveri, the Vennaru and the Puthu Aru, and when the gates are opened floodwater can pass through the other three delta branches to the sea. The ancient irrigation network watered about 69,000 acres, and by the early 20th century the irrigated area had grown to about one million acres. The delta farmers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> have asked the state government to honour Karikala Cholan, to whom the dam is ascribed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kallanai Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sangam poems celebrate Karikala, who defeated a coalition of Cheras and Pandyas at Venni and is credited by tradition with the <b>Kallanai</b>, an early dam across the Kaveri.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-cholas">Early Cholas</a></li></ul></section>
